class:: SensoryDissonance2
summary:: Pairwise sensory roughness of spectral peaks
categories:: UGens>Analysis>Tonal
related:: Guides/MIR2, Classes/HPCP, Classes/HarmonicPeaks

description::
Implements the pairwise Plomp-Levelt curve parametrization described by Sethares (1993 and subsequent author documentation). For two peaks, x = 0.24 * abs(f2-f1) / (0.0207*min(f1,f2)+18.96); roughness is 5 * amplitudeWeight * (exp(-3.51*x)-exp(-5.75*x)). Every unordered pair contributes once. The minimum model weights by the smaller magnitude, while product uses their product. This is an independent equation implementation with bounded peak extraction. Sensory roughness is not an aesthetic or cultural judgement of musical dissonance. Unresolved close peaks, FFT window and peak threshold strongly affect the estimate.



classmethods::
method:: kr
argument:: chain
FFT chain. This analyzer updates when a new frame arrives and holds its output between frames.

argument:: model
Either \minimum or \product amplitude weighting; both use the same Sethares frequency curve.

argument:: maxPeaks
Literal strongest-peak capacity from 2 through 128. Pairwise work grows quadratically.

argument:: normalize
Positive values divide peak magnitudes by the frame maximum before measuring roughness; zero preserves raw FFT magnitude scaling.

argument:: peakThreshold
Minimum local-peak magnitude relative to the largest included spectral peak, from 0 through 1. Up to 128 strongest peaks are retained.

argument:: fftSize
Literal power of two from 256 through 8192, matching the FFT buffer. Frequency resolution and first-result latency depend on this size.

returns::
Nonnegative roughness sum; zero for silence or fewer than two peaks. The value is not constrained to 0 through 1.

examples::
SensoryDissonance2 models acoustic roughness based on the Sethares parametrization of the Plomp & Levelt sensory dissonance curves across all pairs of spectral peaks. In this example, it drives an adaptive waveshaping saturator: consonant harmonic chords pass through with warmth and clarity, while crunchy dissonant intervals trigger aggressive saturation and wavefolding. An interactive code::MIR2GUI.scope:: tracks the real-time roughness metric.
code::
(
Routine({
    var path = MIR2GUI.soundPath("piano-chords.wav");
    ~mir2Buffer = if(path.notNil) { Buffer.read(s, path) } { nil };
    ~mir2Bus = Bus.control(s, 1);
    s.sync;
    ~mir2Example = {
        var source = PlayBuf.ar(1, ~mir2Buffer, BufRateScale.kr(~mir2Buffer), loop: 1);
        // For live input instead: source = SoundIn.ar(0);

        var chain = FFT(LocalBuf(2048), source, 0.25);
        var roughness = SensoryDissonance2.kr(chain, model: \minimum, maxPeaks: 32, normalize: 1, fftSize: 2048);
        var drive = (1.0 + (roughness.clip(0, 5) * 4)).lag(0.04);
        var saturated = (source * drive).tanh * 0.3;
        var sound = (source * 0.2) + saturated;

        if(~mir2Bus.notNil) { Out.kr(~mir2Bus, roughness) };
        sound * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.2) ! 2
    }.play;

    ~mir2GUI = MIR2GUI.scope("Sensory Roughness", ~mir2Bus, 0.0, 5.0);
}).play(AppClock);
)
::
code::
(
~mir2Example.free;
if(~mir2GUI.notNil) { ~mir2GUI.close };
if(~mir2Bus.notNil) { ~mir2Bus.free };
if(~mir2Buffer.notNil) { ~mir2Buffer.free };
)
::

